Go to contact and just follow the instructions there and tell us things.
点击联系我们，然后按照那里的指示告诉我们你的故事。

00:54:43.499 --> 00:54:45.028
So here's this week's letter.
所以这是本周的信。

00:54:45.028 --> 00:54:46.029
Hi, Josh and Craig.
嗨，乔什和克雷格。

00:54:46.030 --> 00:54:46.892
I've watched him.
我看过这部剧。

00:54:46.892 --> 00:54:55.309
So many times I've come to think of it as a best friend, a pillar I can turn to throughout my life for laughs, joy, comfort, understanding, and a shoulder to lean on.
看过太多次了，我开始把它当成我最好的朋友，是我一生中在遇到欢笑、快乐、安慰、理解以及需要一个可以依靠的肩膀时可以求助的支柱。

00:54:55.869 --> 00:55:04.954
Whenever I talk with my sister, my actual best friend and soulmate in many ways, about a hard time either one of us is going through, I frequently relate the situation to him yum.
每当我跟我姐姐——她实际上是我最好的朋友，在许多方面也是我的灵魂伴侣——谈论我们俩正在经历的艰难时期时，我经常会把这种情况跟老爸老妈联系起来。

00:55:04.954 --> 00:55:11.704
I'll say things like, it's like when Marshall and Lily got in that fight, or remember when Ted thought this was the greatest tragedy?
我会说类似这样的话：“这就像马歇尔和莉莉大吵一架的那次”，或者“还记得泰德认为那是最大的悲剧的时候吗？”

00:55:11.704 --> 00:55:19.469
It's a timeless show that I will always go back to simply because it's given me so many answers in my life, even when the answer was, there is no answer.
这是一部永恒的剧集，我总是会重温它，仅仅因为它给了我生活中那么多的答案，哪怕当时的答案是：根本没有答案。

00:55:19.469 --> 00:55:20.067
I love that.
我太喜欢这样了。

00:55:20.067 --> 00:55:28.029
Sometimes I'll be feeling an intense emotion and pick a specific episode to watch because I know it'll help me feel validated and give me a greater perspective.
有时我会有强烈的某种情绪，然后挑出特定的一集来看，因为我知道这会让我觉得自己被理解，并给我带来更广阔的视角。

00:55:28.029 --> 00:55:40.469
For instance, when I wanted to tell my boyfriend that I was in love with him, but was having a tough time getting it out, I watched first time in New York over and over again, just so I could realize I'm not alone and other people deal with this too.
例如，当我想要告诉男朋友我爱他，但又很难说出口时，我一遍又一遍地看《纽约初相识》，就是为了让我意识到我并不孤单，其他人也会面临这样的情况。

00:55:40.469 --> 00:55:44.301
I even went so far as to play the episode for my boyfriend before I told him.
我甚至在向男朋友表白之前，把那一集放给他看了。

00:55:44.301 --> 00:55:44.801
Oh my God.
天哪。

00:55:44.801 --> 00:55:57.589
We used to do this thing before we had said it to each other, where we'd be gazing into each other's eyes and we'd go tight-lipped because neither of us was ready to say it to the other, but we both knew we wanted to say it to each other and we just didn't.
在我们向彼此表白之前，我们曾有过这样一幕：我们会凝视着彼此的眼睛，然后紧闭着嘴唇，因为我们俩谁都还没准备好对对方说出口，但我们俩都知道自己很想对对方说，只是没有说。

00:55:57.589 --> 00:56:03.859
I showed him first time in New York and he knew I was trying to tell him I loved him, but all that could come out was falafel.
我给他看了《纽约初相识》，他知道我是想告诉他我爱他，但结果他嘴里只能吐出“沙三宝（Falafel）”这个词。

00:56:03.859 --> 00:56:07.094
Sadly, my boyfriend and I broke up a little over four months ago.
遗憾的是，我和男朋友在四个多月前分手了。

00:56:07.094 --> 00:56:13.264
I'm still madly in love with him and being able to turn to him for guidance during this emotional time has been such a gift.
我依然疯狂地爱着他，在这个充满情绪的时期，能够向这部剧寻求指导真是一份巨大的礼物。

00:56:13.264 --> 00:56:16.001
I'm still hoping we'll work our way back to each other.
我仍然希望我们能重新回到彼此身边。

00:56:16.001 --> 00:56:23.167
I at least can hold out hope that my yellow umbrella is out there and I just need to wait a little longer because he's coming as fast as he can.
我至少可以抱着这样的希望：我的黄色雨伞就在外面，我只需要再等一会儿，因为他正以最快的速度向我奔来。

00:56:23.167 --> 00:56:26.352
Thank you for the gift of your art that has shaped my life, Ali.
感谢你们创作的艺术点亮了我的生活，阿里。

00:56:28.130 --> 00:56:29.170
Well, that's beautiful.
嗯，太美好了。

00:56:30.849 --> 00:56:31.715
Thank you, Ali.
谢谢你，阿里。

00:56:31.715 --> 00:56:32.215
Yeah.
对。

00:56:32.215 --> 00:56:50.325
You know, it made me think about something that I put this actually in liberal arts and it's attributed to David Foster Wallace, but a lot of stuff is attributed to him that he maybe this is said, but wasn't, didn't originate with him, but it was something along the lines of fiction is the antidote to loneliness.
你知道，这让我想起了一件事，我其实把它放进《自由艺术》里了，这归功于大卫·福斯特·华莱士，但很多话都被归功于他，也许这话是他说的，但也许不是，不是他原创的，但大意是虚构作品是孤独的解药。

00:56:50.325 --> 00:57:01.630
And it's like that James Baldwin thing, you know, they said he felt so alone until he read Dostoyevsky and all these other great authors and realized, oh, they thought they had these feelings too.
这就像詹姆斯·鲍德温的那句话，你知道的，他们说他曾经感到如此孤独，直到他读了陀思妥耶夫斯基和所有这些伟大的作家，然后意识到，哦，原来他们也有过这些感受。

00:57:01.630 --> 00:57:03.245
They had these thoughts too.
他们也有过这些想法。

00:57:03.245 --> 00:57:42.581
I'm actually connected to humanity because, and this is another Wallace thing, like he says, we're all marooned in our own skulls and it's tricky to know like am I experiencing like am I alone here like like do other people feel an ache and hurt and yearn and laugh and is it all this in there's something about making art where you can see like oh it's such a relief when you see like oh yeah Robin has such a hard time saying she loves her boyfriend and so do I oh my god yeah what a relief you know I'm less alone in the world and I think that's one of the real gifts of of how I met your mother and all All great things, all great art and story, let's you know you're not alone.
我实际上是与人类相连的，因为——这也是华莱士的观点——他说我们都被困在自己的头脑中，很难弄清楚：我现在的经历是独一无二的吗？我在这里是孤独的吗？其他人也会感到痛苦、心碎、渴望和欢笑吗？这一切都在这里，而艺术创作的神奇之处就在于你能看到，哦，当你发现“哦，原来罗宾也很难对男友说出‘我爱你’，而我也是”时，那种如释重负的感觉真是太棒了。天哪，没错，多么解脱啊，你知道我在世界上不再孤单了。我认为这是《老爸老妈的浪漫史》以及所有伟大的事物、所有伟大的艺术和故事带来的真正礼物之一，它们让你知道你并不孤单。

00:57:42.369 --> 00:57:44.210
Yeah, that's beautifully said.
是的，这话讲得太好了。

00:57:45.650 --> 00:57:47.409
And I'm just going to say it, guys.
我要把它说出来，伙计们。

00:57:47.409 --> 00:57:49.489
Falafel, Falafel.
沙三宝（Falafel），沙三宝（Falafel）。

